/*! \page install_compile_page Compilation

\ref install_configure_page (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref install_inout_page     (Next)
<BR>

This page contains instructions for compiling the PSCF package using 
default settings, after completing all required 
\ref install_compile_page "preliminary steps". We have divided the
instructions into a set of instructions for compiling and installing 
CPU and GPU-enabled executables. 

\section install_compile_CPU_sec Compiling C++ code (CPU hardware)

We recommend that users first attempt to compile only the CPU executables, 
pscf_1d and pscf_rpc.

Compilation of GPU-enabled code is disabled by default.  To compile only
the pscf_1d and pscf_rpc CPU programs, you may simply enter
\code
    make all
\endcode
from the PSCF directory immediately after running the configure script.
If successful, this will install executable files named pscf_1d and
pscf_rpc in the PSCF bin/ directory.

Compiling all the PSCF C++ code takes some time (e.g., 2 to 3 minutes 
on a 2020 Mac air laptop with an M1 chip). This can be completed more 
quickly on a multi-core machine by using the -j option of the "make" 
command to use multiple CPU cores to compile multiple different C++ 
files in parallel.  The argument of the -j option is the number of 
cores that should be used, if available. For example, you could enter
\code
   make -j8 all
\endcode
to use all 8 CPU cores of an 8-core computer.

Entering "make all" or "make -jn all" from the PSCF root directory,
as described above, will create a large number of intermediate object
(*.o), dependency (*.d) and library (*.a) files in subdirectories of
the PSCF bld/ directory, in addition to executable files that are
created in the PSCF bin/ directory.

\section install_compile_GPU_sec Compiling CUDA C++ Code (GPU hardware)

The GPU-enabled pscf_rpg executable can only be compiled on computers 
that have an NVIDIA CUDA development kit installed, and can only be run 
on machines that have an appropriate CUDA-capable NVIDIA GPU and CUDA 
run-time software installed.  Before compiling pscf_rpg, the user must 
first enable compilation of CUDA code and set the appropriate target 
GPU architecture, as described below.  The following instructions assume 
that you have already completed  required preliminary steps to install
dependencies and set environment variables:

   - **Enable compilation of GPU-enabled executables:**
     To enable compilation of CUDA code on a machine that is set up to allow 
     this, enter
     \code
     ./setopts -u1
     \endcode
     from the PSCF root directory. If you later want to disable compilation 
     of CUDA code, instead enter
     \code
     ./setopts -u0
     \endcode
     Commands issued by the setopts script modify variables in configuration 
     files that control subsequent behavior of the build system.  Usage of 
     the setopts script is discussed in more detail 
     \ref install_setopts_page "here".

     To check if CUDA compilation is currently enabled, change directory (cd)
     to the PSCF bld/ directory, and enter
     \code
     ./setopts -q
     \endcode
     from there. This should yield a report that indicates (among other 
     things) whether the "CUDA" option is currently set to be enabled (ON) 
     or disabled (OFF) for code built in the bld/ directory (which is the 
     default build directory).

   - **Set the target GPU architecture:**
     CUDA C++ must be compiled for a particular target GPU architecture.  
     To set the target architecture to correspond to the GPU on your 
     computer, you must change directory to PSCF root directory and issue 
     a command of the form
     \code
     ./setopts -a [architecture id]
     \endcode
     where [architecture id] denotes a string that identifies the 
     architecture of the GPU for which you are compiling. Valid values of 
     this string have the form "sm_IJ", where IJ denotes a integer string 
     that represents the NVIDIA "compute capability" for the target GPU. 
     You can look up the compute capabability for the type of NVIDIA GPU 
     installed on your computer by consulting the 
     <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus"> NVIDIA developer 
     documentation</a>. The compute capability for an NVIDIA GPU is a 
     number of the form I.J in which I is an integer major version number 
     and J is an integer minor version number. The architecture id string 
     of the form sm_IJ omits the dot between the major and minor version
     numbers.

     For example, an NVIDIA A100 GPU has a compute capability of 8.0.  To 
     select this as the target architecture, one would enter
     \code
     ./setopts -a sm_80
     \endcode
     from the PSCF root directory, using "sm_80" as the architecture id 
     string.

     By default, the target architecture is set to compute capability 8.0, 
     or sm_80, as appropriate for an A100 GPU.

   - **Compile all programs (including pscf_rpg):**
     After enabling CUDA compilation and setting the appropriate
     architecture, you can compile all programs, including pscf_rpg, by
     simply re-entering
     \code
     make all
     \endcode
     from within the PSCF root directory. As when compiling pure C++ 
     code, one may use multiple cores to compile by instead entering 
     "make -jn all", where "n" is the number of CPU cores to be used.

If successful, the "make all" command will install an executable file named 
pscf_rpg in the PSCF bin/ directory, in addition to the files named pscf_1d 
and pscf_rpc that are created by compiling only C++ code.

Several of the above steps are discussed in more detail in subsequent pages.

\section user_overview_inout_sec Out-of-source vs. in-source compilation

The above instructions explain how to perform an "out-of-source" build in
which intermediate files created during compilation process are placed in
the PSCF bld/ sub-directory tree. One can instead perform an "in-source"
build in which these files are instead placed in the PSCF src/ directory
that also contains all C++ and CUDA source code files. The option of
in-source compilation is discussed in more detail \ref install_inout_page
"here".  We recommend out-of-source compilation for users who are not
actively working on development. Some programmers may prefer to use
in-source compilation during development.

<BR>
